  "allopurinol (xanthine oxidase inhibition) blocks azathioprine/6-MP inactivation; profound myelosuppression. Management: avoid; if essential, use 25-33% azathioprine dose with weekly CBC. This interaction can be fatal - check before every co-prescription.",
  "Allopurinol plus candesartan: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus captopril: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus enalapril: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus irbesartan: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus lisinopril: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus losartan: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "allopurinol can raise MTX exposure modestly (shared XO pathway, low-dose MTX mostly unaffected). Management: monitor CBC/LFTs; usually acceptable in rheumatology with monitoring.",
  "Allopurinol plus olmesartan: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus perindopril: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus ramipril: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus sacubitril+valsartan: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus telmisartan: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol plus valsartan: increased risk of severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN) and hypersensitivity, especially with renal impairment or HLA-B*5801 positivity. Management: start allopurinol low, stop at first mucosal/rash signs.",
  "Allopurinol may potentiate warfarin (CYP-mediated); INR rise possible. Management: check INR 1-2 weeks after starting allopurinol.",
  "alprazolam plus amisulpride: additive sedation and psychomotor impairment; fall risk. Management: avoid chronic benzodiazepine co-use, prefer non-sedating strategies, monitor elderly.",
  "alprazolam plus aripiprazole: additive sedation and psychomotor impairment; fall risk. Management: avoid chronic benzodiazepine co-use, prefer non-sedating strategies, monitor elderly.",
