  "amiodarone inhibits CYP2C9, the main clearance path of acenocoumarol; exposure and anticoagulant/anticonvulsant effect rise within days. Management: INR (for warfarin/acenocoumarol) or phenytoin levels before co-prescription; reduce dose 25-50%, monitor for bleeding or nystagmus/ataxia; avoid the combination when an alternative antimicrobial exists.",
  "Both amiodarone (antiarrhythmic) and amisulpride (antipsychotic) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both amiodarone (antiarrhythmic) and amitriptyline (antidepressant) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both amiodarone (antiarrhythmic) and artemether+lumefantrine (antimalarial) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Amiodarone inhibits CYP3A4 raising atorvastatin levels; myopathy risk. Management: cap simvastatin at 20 mg/day, lovastatin at 40 mg/day, atorvastatin at 20 mg/day with amiodarone; monitor CK.",
