  "citalopram and sumatriptan both increase central serotonergic tone; risk of serotonin syndrome (agitation, tremor, hyperreflexia, hyperthermia, autonomic instability). Management: avoid the combination; if unavoidable start low, watch for early syndrome signs in the first 2 weeks, stop the serotonergic agent and support symptomatically if it develops.",
  "citalopram and tapentadol both increase central serotonergic tone; risk of serotonin syndrome (agitation, tremor, hyperreflexia, hyperthermia, autonomic instability). Management: avoid the combination; if unavoidable start low, watch for early syndrome signs in the first 2 weeks, stop the serotonergic agent and support symptomatically if it develops.",
  "citalopram and tramadol both increase central serotonergic tone; risk of serotonin syndrome (agitation, tremor, hyperreflexia, hyperthermia, autonomic instability). Management: avoid the combination; if unavoidable start low, watch for early syndrome signs in the first 2 weeks, stop the serotonergic agent and support symptomatically if it develops.",
  "citalopram and tramadol+paracetamol both increase central serotonergic tone; risk of serotonin syndrome (agitation, tremor, hyperreflexia, hyperthermia, autonomic instability). Management: avoid the combination; if unavoidable start low, watch for early syndrome signs in the first 2 weeks, stop the serotonergic agent and support symptomatically if it develops.",
  "Both citalopram (antidepressant) and venlafaxine (antidepressant) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both citalopram (antidepressant) and voriconazole (azole antifungal) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "citalopram impairs platelet serotonin uptake, adding to warfarin anticoagulation; bleeding/INR rise possible. Management: monitor INR after SSRI start/stop, GI protection, educate on bruising.",
  "citalopram and zolmitriptan both increase central serotonergic tone; risk of serotonin syndrome (agitation, tremor, hyperreflexia, hyperthermia, autonomic instability). Management: avoid the combination; if unavoidable start low, watch for early syndrome signs in the first 2 weeks, stop the serotonergic agent and support symptomatically if it develops.",
  "clarithromycin raises warfarin exposure/INR (CYP3A4 for clarithromycin/erythromycin; gut-flora and P-gp effects for azithromycin); bleeding risk. Management: INR check 3-5 days after starting the antibiotic, watch for bruising; azithromycin is the preferred macrolide if warfarin cannot be held.",
  "clarithromycin (CYP3A4/P-gp inhibition) increases apixaban exposure and bleeding risk. Management: prefer an alternative anticoagulant-dosing plan; avoid strong dual inhibitors outright, halve dose with strong single-pathway inhibition only if physician directed; watch for bleeding signs.",
  "clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4, raising atorvastatin plasma levels; risk of myopathy and rare rhabdomyolysis is dose-dependent. Management: avoid with simvastatin/lovastatin; for atorvastatin cap at 20 mg/day with strong inhibitors; warn on muscle pain and dark urine, check CK if symptomatic.",
  "Both clarithromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and clozapine (antipsychotic) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "clarithromycin raises colchicine levels via CYP3A4/P-glycoprotein inhibition; neuromuscular toxicity and bone-marrow suppression reported, lethal in renal impairment. Management: avoid prolonged use together; for gout flares prefer NSAID or steroid; reduce colchicine dose by half and monitor for neuropathy if co-use is unavoidable.",
  "Both clarithromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and cotrimoxazole (cardiovascular/other) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4 metabolism of cyclosporine, causing sharp exposure rise with nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity and (tacrolimus) diabetes risk. Management: avoid initiation together; if unavoidable reduce calcineurin-inhibitor dose 50-75%, check trough levels in 2-3 days, monitor creatinine and potassium.",
  "clarithromycin increases domperidone levels through CYP3A4 inhibition (QT/haematologic/toxicity concerns). Management: reduce the lower-risk drug dose where feasible, ECG if QT-relevant, monitor CBC for imatinib; avoid domperidone with strong inhibitors.",
  "clarithromycin slows theophylline clearance; nausea and tachyarrhythmia risk. Management: monitor level or reduce dose ~30% during the antibiotic course.",
