  "aceclofenac blunts enalapril effect (prostaglandin/bradykinin pathways) and can precipitate AKI in volume-depleted patients. Management: lowest NSAID dose briefly, hydrate, check creatinine and BP after NSAID start; avoid chronic NSAIDs in CKD patients on RAAS blockade.",
  "aspirin blunts enalapril effect (prostaglandin/bradykinin pathways) and can precipitate AKI in volume-depleted patients. Management: lowest NSAID dose briefly, hydrate, check creatinine and BP after NSAID start; avoid chronic NSAIDs in CKD patients on RAAS blockade.",
  "celecoxib blunts enalapril effect (prostaglandin/bradykinin pathways) and can precipitate AKI in volume-depleted patients. Management: lowest NSAID dose briefly, hydrate, check creatinine and BP after NSAID start; avoid chronic NSAIDs in CKD patients on RAAS blockade.",
  "enalapril plus chlorthalidone is a standard BP/HF combination but can over-drop BP and worsen renal perfusion with volume loss. Management: titrate gradually, check standing BP, creatinine and K+ (opposite-direction effects) after initiation.",
  "Trimethoprim (in cotrimoxazole) acts like an amiloride; with enalapril life-threatening hyperkalemia can develop within days, especially in the elderly or CKD. Management: prefer an alternative antibiotic; if used, check K+ in 3-5 days, avoid in eGFR < 30.",
  "diclofenac blunts enalapril effect (prostaglandin/bradykinin pathways) and can precipitate AKI in volume-depleted patients. Management: lowest NSAID dose briefly, hydrate, check creatinine and BP after NSAID start; avoid chronic NSAIDs in CKD patients on RAAS blockade.",
  "enalapril plus eplerenone both raise serum potassium (reduced aldosterone effect plus potassium excretion block); hyperkalemia risk, dangerous with renal impairment or diabetes. Management: check K+ and creatinine at 1-2 weeks and after dose changes, avoid other K-raising drugs, salt substitutes off, hold if K+ > 5.5.",
  "etoricoxib blunts enalapril effect (prostaglandin/bradykinin pathways) and can precipitate AKI in volume-depleted patients. Management: lowest NSAID dose briefly, hydrate, check creatinine and BP after NSAID start; avoid chronic NSAIDs in CKD patients on RAAS blockade.",
