  "Trimethoprim plus eplerenone markedly impairs K+ excretion; life-threatening hyperkalemia risk. Management: avoid the combination; check K+ within 3-5 days if unavoidable, stop if K+ > 5.5.",
  "eplerenone may raise digoxin levels slightly and its hyperkalemia can alter digoxin effect. Management: monitor digoxin level, K+ and ECG; educate on digoxin toxicity signs.",
  "erythromycin raises warfarin exposure/INR (CYP3A4 for clarithromycin/erythromycin; gut-flora and P-gp effects for azithromycin); bleeding risk. Management: INR check 3-5 days after starting the antibiotic, watch for bruising; azithromycin is the preferred macrolide if warfarin cannot be held.",
  "erythromycin (CYP3A4/P-gp inhibition) increases apixaban exposure and bleeding risk. Management: prefer an alternative anticoagulant-dosing plan; avoid strong dual inhibitors outright, halve dose with strong single-pathway inhibition only if physician directed; watch for bleeding signs.",
  "erythromycin inhibits CYP3A4, raising atorvastatin plasma levels; risk of myopathy and rare rhabdomyolysis is dose-dependent. Management: avoid with simvastatin/lovastatin; for atorvastatin cap at 20 mg/day with strong inhibitors; warn on muscle pain and dark urine, check CK if symptomatic.",
  "erythromycin raises colchicine levels via CYP3A4/P-glycoprotein inhibition; neuromuscular toxicity and bone-marrow suppression reported, lethal in renal impairment. Management: avoid prolonged use together; for gout flares prefer NSAID or steroid; reduce colchicine dose by half and monitor for neuropathy if co-use is unavoidable.",
  "erythromycin inhibits CYP3A4 metabolism of cyclosporine, causing sharp exposure rise with nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity and (tacrolimus) diabetes risk. Management: avoid initiation together; if unavoidable reduce calcineurin-inhibitor dose 50-75%, check trough levels in 2-3 days, monitor creatinine and potassium.",
  "erythromycin increases domperidone levels through CYP3A4 inhibition (QT/haematologic/toxicity concerns). Management: reduce the lower-risk drug dose where feasible, ECG if QT-relevant, monitor CBC for imatinib; avoid domperidone with strong inhibitors.",
  "erythromycin slows theophylline clearance; nausea and tachyarrhythmia risk. Management: monitor level or reduce dose ~30% during the antibiotic course.",
  "Both erythromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and escitalopram (antidepressant) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both erythromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and fluconazole (azole antifungal) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
