  "Both erythromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and fluoxetine (antidepressant) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both erythromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and fluvoxamine (antidepressant) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both erythromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and granisetron (gastrointestinal) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both erythromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and haloperidol (antipsychotic) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both erythromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and hydroxychloroquine (antimalarial) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both erythromycin (macrolide antibiotic) and hydroxyzine (gastrointestinal) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "erythromycin increases imatinib levels through CYP3A4 inhibition (QT/haematologic/toxicity concerns). Management: reduce the lower-risk drug dose where feasible, ECG if QT-relevant, monitor CBC for imatinib; avoid domperidone with strong inhibitors.",
