  "fentanyl plus olanzapine: additive sedation, cognitive impairment and fall risk. Management: lowest doses, avoid driving, monitor in the elderly.",
  "fentanyl plus pheniramine: additive sedation, cognitive impairment and fall risk. Management: lowest doses, avoid driving, monitor in the elderly.",
  "fentanyl plus pregabalin: additive CNS/respiratory depression; sedation and fall risk. Management: titrate carefully, avoid in the elderly where possible, educate on driving and sedation.",
  "fentanyl plus promethazine: additive sedation, cognitive impairment and fall risk. Management: lowest doses, avoid driving, monitor in the elderly.",
  "fentanyl plus quetiapine: additive sedation, cognitive impairment and fall risk. Management: lowest doses, avoid driving, monitor in the elderly.",
  "fentanyl plus risperidone: additive sedation, cognitive impairment and fall risk. Management: lowest doses, avoid driving, monitor in the elderly.",
  "fentanyl plus zolpidem additively depresses respiration and consciousness; overdose death risk especially in the elderly or with sleep apnea. Management: avoid co-prescription; if essential use lowest doses for shortest time, warn patient/family, never combine with alcohol; consider naloxone availability.",
  "fentanyl plus zopiclone additively depresses respiration and consciousness; overdose death risk especially in the elderly or with sleep apnea. Management: avoid co-prescription; if essential use lowest doses for shortest time, warn patient/family, never combine with alcohol; consider naloxone availability.",
  "ferrous ascorbate+folate chelates levodopa and reduces absorption; parkinsonian control can slip. Management: separate doses by 2 hours; monitor motor response.",
  "ferrous ascorbate+folate reduces levothyroxine absorption (chelation or acid-dependent dissolution); hypothyroid drift over weeks. Management: take levothyroxine fasting and separate ferrous ascorbate+folate by at least 4 hours; recheck TSH 6-8 weeks after any change.",
  "ferrous ascorbate+folate chelates mycophenolate (MPA) reducing exposure; rejection risk. Management: separate administration by 2+ hours; monitor MPA levels if available.",
  "ferrous sulphate chelates levodopa and reduces absorption; parkinsonian control can slip. Management: separate doses by 2 hours; monitor motor response.",
  "ferrous sulphate reduces levothyroxine absorption (chelation or acid-dependent dissolution); hypothyroid drift over weeks. Management: take levothyroxine fasting and separate ferrous sulphate by at least 4 hours; recheck TSH 6-8 weeks after any change.",
  "ferrous sulphate chelates mycophenolate (MPA) reducing exposure; rejection risk. Management: separate administration by 2+ hours; monitor MPA levels if available.",
  "flavoxate plus antiparkinsonian anticholinergic: additive anticholinergic burden (dry mouth, urinary retention, confusion, precipitates glaucoma). Management: minimize duplication, screen elderly for cognitive effects.",
  "flucloxacillin reduces MTX tubular secretion; level rise. Management: monitor CBC and mucositis during co-use, especially high-dose MTX.",
  "fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9, the main clearance path of acenocoumarol; exposure and anticoagulant/anticonvulsant effect rise within days. Management: INR (for warfarin/acenocoumarol) or phenytoin levels before co-prescription; reduce dose 25-50%, monitor for bleeding or nystagmus/ataxia; avoid the combination when an alternative antimicrobial exists.",
  "fluconazole (CYP3A4/P-gp inhibition) increases apixaban exposure and bleeding risk. Management: prefer an alternative anticoagulant-dosing plan; avoid strong dual inhibitors outright, halve dose with strong single-pathway inhibition only if physician directed; watch for bleeding signs.",
  "fluconazole inhibits CYP3A4, raising atorvastatin plasma levels; risk of myopathy and rare rhabdomyolysis is dose-dependent. Management: avoid with simvastatin/lovastatin; for atorvastatin cap at 20 mg/day with strong inhibitors; warn on muscle pain and dark urine, check CK if symptomatic.",
  "fluconazole raises colchicine levels via CYP3A4/P-glycoprotein inhibition; neuromuscular toxicity and bone-marrow suppression reported, lethal in renal impairment. Management: avoid prolonged use together; for gout flares prefer NSAID or steroid; reduce colchicine dose by half and monitor for neuropathy if co-use is unavoidable.",
  "fluconazole inhibits CYP3A4 metabolism of cyclosporine, causing sharp exposure rise with nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity and (tacrolimus) diabetes risk. Management: avoid initiation together; if unavoidable reduce calcineurin-inhibitor dose 50-75%, check trough levels in 2-3 days, monitor creatinine and potassium.",
  "fluconazole increases domperidone levels through CYP3A4 inhibition (QT/haematologic/toxicity concerns). Management: reduce the lower-risk drug dose where feasible, ECG if QT-relevant, monitor CBC for imatinib; avoid domperidone with strong inhibitors.",
  "Both fluconazole (azole antifungal) and flunarizine (cardiovascular/other) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
  "Both fluconazole (azole antifungal) and fluoxetine (antidepressant) prolong cardiac repolarization via IKr (hERG) channel blockade; additive QTc prolongation and torsades-de-pointes risk, higher with hypokalemia, bradycardia, female sex or structural heart disease. Management: baseline ECG with QTc before co-prescription, correct potassium/magnesium, prefer non-QT alternatives; avoid in known long-QT syndrome.",
