  "itraconazole inhibits CYP2C9/3A4 raising INR with acenocoumarol. Management: INR checks 2-3x/week after azole start; reduce warfarin dose; educate on bleeding signs.",
  "itraconazole (CYP3A4/P-gp inhibition) increases apixaban exposure and bleeding risk. Management: prefer an alternative anticoagulant-dosing plan; avoid strong dual inhibitors outright, halve dose with strong single-pathway inhibition only if physician directed; watch for bleeding signs.",
  "itraconazole inhibits CYP3A4, raising atorvastatin plasma levels; risk of myopathy and rare rhabdomyolysis is dose-dependent. Management: avoid with simvastatin/lovastatin; for atorvastatin cap at 20 mg/day with strong inhibitors; warn on muscle pain and dark urine, check CK if symptomatic.",
  "itraconazole raises colchicine levels via CYP3A4/P-glycoprotein inhibition; neuromuscular toxicity and bone-marrow suppression reported, lethal in renal impairment. Management: avoid prolonged use together; for gout flares prefer NSAID or steroid; reduce colchicine dose by half and monitor for neuropathy if co-use is unavoidable.",
  "itraconazole inhibits CYP3A4 metabolism of cyclosporine, causing sharp exposure rise with nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity and (tacrolimus) diabetes risk. Management: avoid initiation together; if unavoidable reduce calcineurin-inhibitor dose 50-75%, check trough levels in 2-3 days, monitor creatinine and potassium.",
  "itraconazole increases domperidone levels through CYP3A4 inhibition (QT/haematologic/toxicity concerns). Management: reduce the lower-risk drug dose where feasible, ECG if QT-relevant, monitor CBC for imatinib; avoid domperidone with strong inhibitors.",
